Essentially the latter. Money is built on agreements that are made using
language.

[...]
|> > Language, like tool-making, can be seen to be rooted in
|> >behavior we clearly class as instinctual, but the same cannot be said
|> >for money.
|> 
|> As in, a feral child will make noises but won't make trades? I think you're
|> stretching it, and I'm not sure why. What do you want to use this
|> distinction for? 

I also distinguish between "trade" and the use of money. Money is _quite
literally_ more abstract than barter; the coin or bead or note _stands
for_ the output of some other transaction somewhere else, without anyone
needing to recall the details of that other transaction.



Even if money is "a few levels of abstraction higher and,
|> unlike language, has no clear precedent in our instinctual heritage," there
|> is some merit to thinking about its similarity to language. Neither was
|> invented nor found, both are subjected to, but not controlled by, the
|> conscious efforts of individuals. If you find similar phenomena at
|> different levels of abstraction, isn't that interesting?

It's interesting because it's so common ;-) If you take a sequence of
advanced algebra classes, each will build on what went before, but the
same types of relations and properties are being exploited. When you
get to very high levels of abstraction, it is sometimes shocking to
look back down the stack and see how many concepts you have subsumed.

|> >we'll never have the evidence;[. . .]
|> >the theories you cite above, while
|> >appealing to common sense, can't be empirically supported.
|> 
|> What, no such thing as Archeology? B-) Okay, so no one has a video tape of
|> the first money transaction. So what? Does that mean we can know nothing
|> about it? That certain speculations can't be eliminated/falsified? That
|> talk on this subject is just a waste?

It's tempting ;-)

We started with a contention by Tim Starr that money, language,
rights, etc. were all the same in that they were not "inventions". I
pointed out that that is more obvious for some of the lumped-together
items than others, in particular I don't think anyone can prove that
money was _not_ invented. The bow was invented, apparently fairly
recently. The spear and axe were apparently invented before H.
sapiens. Language must have evolved from something like what we see in
other primate spp., in response to increases in the complexity of
group behaviors such as hunting, tool-making, and dealing with other
tribes. There's a gap there in our understanding between what we can
observe of language evolution, which is fully-formed languages
changing, and what we can surmise about the bootstrap process.

Also with the origin of money -- archaeology shows us early forms of
accounting, long-distance trade, and coinage in use at an early date,
by people we would call "civilized" (literally! they built cities),
and before that there's a gap. We can speculate about how humans
bridged that gap, and maybe by doing so narrow down the possibilities,
but I'm not hopeful that we can narrow them down to one by
speculation.

How would you go about disproving any hypothesis that involved the
_invention_ of money by some group of people and its subsequent spread
and evolution to and by other groups with similar needs?

And does the fact that something like that can be invented in parallel
by several groups show that it is _not_ an invention? I think not;
after all, that happens today with things that are undeniably invented
artifacts, and we aren't even surprised. "Ideas arise when their time
has come".

I guess I answer "what do I want to do with those distinctions?"  by,
"show that human ingenuity and inventiveness should not be easily
discounted".

