You've seen all the horror stories.  Some have even been posted to this 
list about how there's no work out there and our children are going to die 
like dogs in the dirt because we (elect Democrats/elect Republicans/pass 
NAFTA/defeat NAFTA/conclude the Uruguay Round/let the Uruguay Round 
fail/fail to adequately fund our schools/fund our schools).

Tim's question is phrased incorrectly.  Substitute "high-paying work" for 
"high-paying jobs."  The truth is that there will be as much work as 
people want.  Work is not something created by others.  Work exists 
because people want to obtain the fruits of work.  If there are no people, 
the jobs/work are not there.  If people want jobs -- *really* want them -- 
they are there.  The government can screw things up in a big way for a 
limited period of time (The Great Depression) but its abilities are 
limited these days.

The problem is that people don't really want jobs.  They want income which 
is not the same thing.  When I tell people that anyone who wants to can 
make 50K per annum as a Word for Windows temp on the lobster shift at 
Perry's company in NYC (or some of its competitors who are actually making 
money these days <G>)  they say things like "I don't like to do office 
work" or "There aren't really enough of those jobs to solve New York 
City's unemployment problem."  The first answer means they don't really 
want to work and the second answer is irrelevant since there are enough of 
those jobs available for anyone willing to take them.  Any individual can 
solve their own unemployment with a job that you can get by lying about 
having a high school diploma.  All you have to be able to do is to 
read, write, type, and word process (all of which can be learned by anyone 
of normal intelligence.)

That means BTW that the real "minimum wage" in  NYC is really $16 to $25 
per hour (that being the range earned by WP temps on different shifts). 
Since anyone who wants to can earn that much, if they choose not to the 
cash wage they do earn + their psychic wage from avoiding the rigors of 
the WP temp market must be equal to or higher than the amount that they 
could earn as a temp.

A few years ago, Robert Heinlein said that he had been unable  to get 
anyone to repair a stone wall on his property (near Tim's residence) no 
matter what the price.  One runs into a lot of this these days.

The angst that job seekers suffer today is caused by the fact that the 
employment market is changing.  What people think of as jobs are 
disappearing (and good riddance) and contingent employment (really a 
species of self-employment) is growing.  Learn to love it.

You are not going to experience the following scenario any more:  

Graduate from high school on Friday, spend Friday and Saturday nights 
partying, spend Sunday recovering, roll out of bed Monday morning and 
stagger with your buddies down to the local factory gates to start your 
job, park your brain for the next 40 years.

Many may prefer the leisure that vast wealth (like ours today) makes 
possible to the rigors of work.  That is their choice.  If governments 
weaken they may have to find new patrons for their idleness.  At least 
there is no risk that they will have to sell out and become a Man in a 
Gray Flannel Suit.  

I do believe that anyone of normal intelligence who wants to work can and 
will be able to find "well paying" work (if not a job).

