Subject: The Evidence of the Senses

I think Ratha has an interesting point here (follows), let me restate to
see if I have got it right, and then elaborate slightly:

>                                                         What if
> someone were to upload into one such computer? What kind of computing
> power would it take to perform such a reality simulation on _it_? Would
> that require--attotech? 7:) Seriously, would it become in fact
> impossible?

If I understand correctly, Ratha is suggesting induction on the brain-in-
a-vat:  We take a processor of whatever level of capability is required to
provide "good" VR for a human mind -- call this a "level 1" computer.
We then upload someone into a level one computer, and ask what level of
capability is required to provide "good" VR for the newly-uploaded entity --
call this a "level 2" computer.  We then upload someone into a level 2
computer, and so on ...

At least two possibilities occur to me.  (1) There exists an N for which
a "level N" computer has the same capability as a "level N+1" computer.
I think this is equivalent to saying that the amount of processing power
required to provide "good" VR is finite, even if if the VR is to be
provided to a human being uploaded into an arbitrarily powerful processor.
(2) For all N, a "level N+1" computer is more capable than a "level N"
one.  (Actually, (2a) There's an upper bound to the capabilities thereby
defined, and (2b) there isn't).

This is an interesting line of reasoning, which in my opinion weakens the
argument that "real" reality is ultimately indistinguishable from 
brain-in-a-vat reality (or however you choose to summarize this thread).
I am easily convinced that my brain in a vat could be deceived by a
competent computer -- say, Perry's Jovian-sized one.  (Or on a bad
day, probably by a Nintendo games machine.)  I shall be curious to see
whether anyone comes up with an argument that there is a fixed-capability
machine that could deceive any uploaded human, no matter what processor
was the destination of the upload.

                                  -- Jay Freeman

