T. David Burns asks:
>Is there an Extropian epistemology?

You'll have to ask the official high priests of the true Extropian
principles.  But I'd advise against one.  Not only would it make
Extropianism more of an "ism" (which I dislike), but I don't think
there is an obvious big name person whose writings constitute the
best available opinions on epistemology.  And I agree with Perry and
Jamie that such namedropping hinders more than it helps conversations
on this subject.  

For what it's worth, I'd call myself a "computationally-constrained
Bayesian"; we want our beliefs and belief changes to satsify the
Bayesian constraints, but limited computational abilities require us
to approximate, often beyond recognition.

Robin Hanson

