>
>TH-F> The US is neither overpopulated, nor inefficient (just in case someone
>TH-F> might propose this).
>
>Perhaps true. David Suzuki on NOVA asks us that if Americans consume
>30 times the natural resources that thirdworlders do, are we not
>overpopulated? If such is the case then we should 'consider'
>ourselves overpopulated simply because we will soon (?) outstrip
>the available resources. And again (without the facts to present a
>strong supportive argument for the discussion) just how long will
>these resources last? Are they capital or income? I don't know. I
>do think the subject needs discussion.

I dispute the use of the word "consumed".  All that present industry does
is _rearrange_ materials.  The iron, copper, hydrocarbons, etc., that have
been taken out of the ground have not been shipped off-planet or
annihilated by anti-matter; they're still lying around in different
arrangements (scrap yards, atmospheric reserves, and so forth).  This vast
pool of "enriched ores" is a source of copper, iron, etc., that competes
economically with the ones still lying underground; when the price of
mining gets higher than the price of recycling, the market switches (in the
main) to melting down scrap instead of digging up raw ores.  As Julian
Simon has pointed out, though, it's still cheaper, for most uses, to mine
than to recycle, and continues to grow cheaper as mining technologies
improve.  That was the point of his bet with Paul Ehrlich (which he won)
about the prices of various metals going down, not up.

How long will these resources last, you ask?  From 1000 to 30,000 years,
according to the U. S. Geological Survey (whose estimates, as you may
guess, lean toward conservative).  That's raw ores only, without taking
into account the recycling of scrap, or the constant trend toward doing
more with less (what Bucky Fuller called "ephemeralization").  My guess is
that if mining became completely impossible tomorrow (if, say, Vernor Vinge
bobbled the entire interior of the earth from 10 feet deep on down :-), our
civilization would retool to a full-recycling footing, after only a few
years of upheaval, and be back on the road to economic growth.  We don't do
this now, spontaneously, because we don't have to, and we won't until we
have to.

Michael wonders whether Extropians live on capital or income.  By my
lights, these mined ores become capital once mined -- they remain in the
economy and remain productive, to the extent that our industry has use for
them, or they lie "fallow" in scrapyards or atmospheric reserves until we
need them.  The only real consumable is available energy, of which we
receive vastly more each day in the form of sunlight than we can presently
use in a year.  When solar power becomes cheaper than fossil fuel (when the
technology to make proper use of both terrestrial and space solar energy
becomes economically attractive), then our economies will make the shift --
the shift hasn't taken place on a large scale yet because those who shift
early are putting themselves at an economic disadvantage (a temporary one
that will turn into a headstart when the oil runs out -- or into a fatal
mistake when we discover how to make petroleum out of old National
Geographic magazines or some other abundant resource).

Michael's argument seems to accept the PC assumption that industrial
nations don't "deserve" to use energy at 30x the rate in underdeveloped
nations.  What exactly do Americans (and our fellow resource-intensive
industrial nations) do with that 30x in energy throughput?  Well, for one
thing, we're adding to the _intellectual_ capital of the species... I'll
bet a lot more basic research gets done in the U.S. than in Namibia or
Bangladesh.  Intellectual capital is the most important resource at our
disposal anyway -- iron ore is just dirt unless you have the technological
know-how to make use of it.  The Third World isn't using that much energy
because they have no use for it -- they're not doing anything presently for
which they require that much energy.  Until they begin to shoulder more of
their share of the burden for economic production, we can expect them to
continue to purchase power at their present low rate.

Though this change may be soon.  Speaking of David Suzuki and Third World
industrialization, I saw a fascinating segment of Suzuki's program _The
Nature of Things_ last night on Discover, about the budding machine-tool
industry in Ghana.  They profiled a number of Ghanaian entrepreneurs who
are manufacturing drill presses, grain mills, and other minimum industrial
capabilities inside Ghana, rather than importing them (or worse, their
products, like nuts, bolts, and grinding wheels) from abroad.  These
capitalist heroes are creating thousands of jobs in Ghana, providing better
nutrition for Ghanaian children (through the use of grain mills to produce
a "weaning mix" of ground corn, chickpeas, and peanuts, more
nutritionally-complete than traditional foods and, thanks to the grain
mills, cheaper), and, best of all, obtaining the steel for their machines
from -- guess what? -- smelting down old retired trucks and cars.
