Subject: DATA: Credit Cards

My views on the credit card information being discussed here by Romana
and others:

I use a "credit card" that is actually a *debit* card, that is, my
money is transferred out of my account at the time of the transaction.
The card externally looks like a VISA card--it's not even a fancy
"onyx" color--and is issued by a bank in affilitation with my stock
broker. My broker, Dean Witter, calls it an "Active Assets Account,"
and other names cover similar plans elsewhere. There _is_ a $70 or so
annual fee, but this covers an extensive reporting system (reports to
me, that is), phone queries, etc. Generally, this is an acceptable fee
to me.

(For anyone interested, I think most brokerages want to see an account
valued at at least $25K for these "Active Assets Accounts.")


The main downside: no 30-day "float" between the time the transaction
occurs and the time the bill has to be paid (give or take). Not a big
deal at today's interest rates, but if we ever get back to high
inflation, I may switch back to a conventional card (I do have a
couple of "no annual fee" cards, from AT&T, Bank One, and Discover,
but I almost never use them).

The upsides: 

- No monthly bill to pay (hooray!), as the transfers come
directly from my stock market account (more on this below). This means
no missed payments, no delinquent notices--for someone like me, who
hates bills and paperwork, a blessed situation.

- One convenient report for all transactions: all checks, all stock
market gains, all VISA transactions. This makes for a very convenient
tax preparation document. (There are "Big Brother" risks, I suppose,
but once the baleful eye of Big Bro is on you, they can find 10
different accounts as easily as one. I, on the other hand, find it
easier to concentrate on just one account.)

- The "interest rate" is very low, around 5-6%, as any money needed is
automatically lent to me at the margin rates my broker sets. This is
just about the cheapest money one can ever get, barring special
knowledge and connections that someone like Perry may have (Perry
being Perry-- :-} --I expect he'll jump in here and describe how trivial
it is to get 4.7327% APR money....for me, this method of using my
broker is good enough....I don't want to think about moving my money
around on a frequent basis, and so I'm willing to get
less-than-the-best rates.)

- "Limits" on the card are determined by one's margin limits, which
are generally some fraction of one's account/portfolio worth. Modesty
and politeness prevents me from saying anything about this, except to
say that I took great joy in seeing the look of surprise and
consternation on the salesman's face when I casually told him I'd be
paying for my new Ford Explorer with my VISA card.

(The margin interest rate was as good or better than auto financing
deals, with none of the pesky paperwork.)

Interestingly, I could live off my debit card...it's like a wormhole
directly into my stock account (which is what I live off, anyway).
Anywhere in the world, I can get cash from ATMs, larger amounts of
cash at banks affiliated with VISA (I've never tried this, just the
ATM cash), travellers checks (who needs 'em?), and so on. (And even
buying a house on this card is doable...though the monthly payments
would not be easily deductible, and the interest rates could of course
go up.)

And this is effectively shipping cash straight of the country without
declaring it at the border, just as some of the travellers checks
schemes do. (Wiring money out is a long-used method, so I'm not
claiming to have invented anything new...far from it.)

For me, even though I am a privacy fellow traveller (my crypto interests
are more in new structures, not privacy per se), the conveniences of
this card outweigh the dangers implicit in having all my transactions
funnelled through one brokerage house. Your mileage may vary.

